          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Automatic differentiation
            (AD) is a technique for computing (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transpose+matrix"}> transposed
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative"} derivatives
            of functions implemented by computer programs, essentially by applying the chain-rule across program code. It is typically the method of choice for computing derivatives in machine learning and scientific computing because of its efficiency and numerical stability.
          %h2#forward_and_reverse_automatic_differentiation Forward and reverse automatic differentiation
          %p AD works by calculating the (transposed) derivative of a composite program in terms of the (transposed) derivatives of the parts, by using the chain-rule. The distinction between derivatives and transposed derivatives leads to the main distinction in automatic differentiation modes:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em> forward mode AD
                \: this implements the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
                functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                and calculates derivatives by a forward pass to calculate function values (primals), followed by another forward pass to calculate derivative values (tangents); these two forward passes can be interleaved into a single forward pass for efficiency.
            %li
              %p
                %em> reverse mode AD
                \: this implements the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
                functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^*
                and calculates transposed derivatives by a forward pass to calculate function values (primals), followed by a
                %em reverse
                pass (inverting the control flow of the original program) to calculate transposed derivative values (cotangents); these two passes cannot easily be interleaved due to their differing direction.
          %p
            When calculating a derivative of a program that implements a function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:R^n\to R^m
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≫
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \gg m
            and forward mode tends to be more efficient if
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≪
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\ll m
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:R^n\to R
            (e.g. probability density functions) for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            very large, reverse mode AD tends to be the most popular algorithm.
          %h2#combinatory_homomorphic_automatic_differentiation_chad__a_categorical_take_on_ad Combinatory Homomorphic Automatic Differentiation (CHAD) - a categorical take on AD
          %p
            Let us fix some class of categories with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff"} stuff
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            might include
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property"} properties
            like
          %ul
            %li
              closure under finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
              (aka tuples, in programming-speak);
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} Cartesian closure
              (aka higher-order functions);
            %li
              closure under finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
              (aka sum or variant types);
          %p
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            like
          %ul
            %li
              designated objects like
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              (which we think of as real numbers and integers)
            %li
              designated morphisms like
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi sin
                      %mo :
                      %mi R
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sin:R\to R
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi cos
                      %mo :
                      %mi R
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cos: R\to R
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi R
                      %mo ×
                      %mi R
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (+): R\times R\to R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi R
                    %mo ×
                    %mi R
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (*):R\times R\to R
              (which we think of as the corresponding mathematical operations).
          %p
            The idea behind CHAD will be to view forward and reverse AD as the unique structure (stuff) preserving functor (homomorphism of
            = succeed "-categories)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Syn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Syn
            to two suitably chosen
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi CSyn
                  %mi LSyn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{CSyn}LSyn
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi CSyn
                    %msup
                      %mi LSyn
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{CSyn}LSyn^{op}
          %h3#the_source_language The source language
          %p
            Consider the initial
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
